WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:07.140
Film Courage:&nbsp; I was looking at another talented
screenwriter’s site and her name is Shelley

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:08.719
Gustavson (via Shelley Gustavson.com).&nbsp;

00:00:08.719 --> 00:00:17.000
And she says in this one article “There
are stories that you figure out and then write.&nbsp;

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.820
And then there are stories that you figure
out as you write them.”&nbsp;

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:21.820
Any thoughts?

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:22.930
Barrington Smith Seetachitt:&nbsp; Oh…that’s
interesting.&nbsp;

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.050
Stories that you figure out and write.&nbsp;

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.280
Or stories that you figure out as you write
them...Janice Rhoshalle Littlejohn:&nbsp; I think

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:32.910
that’s true both for my work as a journalist
and a screenwriter.&nbsp;

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:39.980
I can go into an interview believing that
it’s going to go one way and it turns out

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:41.810
to be an entirely new thing.&nbsp;

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:46.930
[This] is a little off topic but I remember
when 9/11 happened and I was supposed to have

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.380
interviewed John Schneider at that time.&nbsp;

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.560
He was Superman…Barrington Smith Seetachitt:
Father…Janice Rhoshalle Littlejohn:&nbsp; He

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.500
was the father of Superman.Barrington Smith
Seetachitt:&nbsp; Smallville.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.500
Janice:&nbsp; Thank you.&nbsp;

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.500
For the CW show Smallville.&nbsp;

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.500
And we had been trying to get this thing [the
interview] together.&nbsp;

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:57.020
We had cancelled and he was out of town.&nbsp;

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:01.730
And so I contacted his publicists and she
was like “No, he still wants to do it.”&nbsp;

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:05.060
And it was the day that George Bush was delivering
the prayer after 9/11.&nbsp;

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.100
I was on the freeway and we still hadn’t
heard from one of my cousins who lived in

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.100
New York.&nbsp;

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.120
And so I’m just like weeping in the car
and it was just so stressful.&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.470
So I pull myself together, I go to his home,
his wife lets me in.&nbsp;

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:13.530
He’s upstairs watching the prayer.&nbsp;

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:18.920
I’m trying to get myself composed and he
comes down and I’ve got journalism face

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.850
on and I’m like “Hello, how are you?”
and he’s like “Did you know anybody?”&nbsp;

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:28.120
All I kept thinking was ‘We don’t know
where Barbara is’ and I just broke down.&nbsp;

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:32.840
So what I thought was just going to be this
innocuous personality profile about this actor

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.689
turned into a really interesting revealing
story, not only about the character he was

00:01:36.689 --> 00:01:40.880
playing but what justice and what patriotism
and all of these things meant.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:46.130
So a story that was supposed to be this cute
little personality profile about this actor

00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:48.100
turned into something deeper and richer.&nbsp;

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:55.590
I talked to my editor about whether or not
I should include that I wept on this man’s

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:56.740
white shirt [laughs].&nbsp;

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:59.985
And so it was one of those things that even
now in the work that we’re doing, we had

00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:01.610
a certain focus and idea about it and I think
as we’ve gone through this screenwriting

00:02:01.610 --> 00:02:04.610
workshop in Switzerland and the experiences
we’ve had there, just stepping back from

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:11.234
the screenplay for a couple of weeks and then
coming back and just kind of reevaluating

00:02:11.234 --> 00:02:14.630
what it is that we’re trying to say and
how are we saying it and are we really affecting

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:15.630
not only good story but are we really saying
something with the way that we’re going.&nbsp;

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:16.630
I think it can happen but I think it can definitely
happen both ways.&nbsp;

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:17.630
I was open to it as a journalist because it
was always fun.&nbsp;

00:02:17.630 --> 00:02:21.500
It’s like a discovery when you have preconceptions
of what your story is but then it develops

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.110
into something very different.&nbsp;

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:28.400
I think there is something very exciting about
that and something very freeing about it if

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:33.260
you allow yourself to open to that, it can
go in a direction.&nbsp;

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:38.460
Because I think even to a certain extent the
things that we’ve done at least in this

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:42.950
screenplay have moved further from what I
thought my original idea was.&nbsp;

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:43.950
But it’s so much better.&nbsp;

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:52.060
I’m working on a documentary where I thought
I was going to have a particular story that

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:55.910
was going to go in a particular way and time
and finances (because I don’t have the money

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:57.720
to continue to generate it) once it’s done.&nbsp;

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:02.270
I can see it’s already a different story
because there was a particular timeframe on

00:03:02.270 --> 00:03:03.550
that story that I thought “Oh, I’m going
to get it done and I’m going to get this

00:03:03.550 --> 00:03:05.980
and the personality is going to be great”
and it didn’t turn out that way, you know

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:06.980
[laughs].&nbsp;

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.230
And so looking at how that story can evolve
now it’s going to be a…you know the story

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:11.230
I wanted to tell was great.&nbsp;

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:13.459
This story could be even greater because of
not only my experience in time but because

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:14.459
of new circumstances and new developments
and new undertones and things like that.&nbsp;

00:03:14.459 --> 00:03:19.420
So I think working with something and having
it be what you thought it was going to be

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:21.690
is great but also the discovery of something
new from what you thought you had is also

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:22.690
a wonderful gift.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:23.690
Barrington Smith Seetachitt: &nbsp; I think…yeah…really
if I’m honest, I’m always figuring it

00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:24.690
out as I write.&nbsp;

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:29.464
Even if I go in thinking that I know what
it is, I’m still going to end up figuring

00:03:29.464 --> 00:03:30.464
it out as I go.&nbsp;

00:03:30.464 --> 00:03:31.464
Yeah….[laughs].&nbsp;

00:03:31.464 --> 00:03:33.220
I have inside-out writing and outside-in writing
and the outside-in will tend to be something

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.580
where there is kind of an external story.&nbsp;

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.571
And so you’ve got the container a little
bit and you’re figuring out all of the ways

00:03:38.571 --> 00:03:40.989
that you’re furnishing and arranging what’s
in that container and generating some new

00:03:40.989 --> 00:03:41.989
stuff.&nbsp;

00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:44.090
And occasionally I have the luxury of doing
kind of what I call the inside-out where you

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:45.090
just have almost nothing, where you woke up
in the middle of the night with an image in

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:46.200
your head or just this kind of very fragmentary
thing like a grain of sand and then it’s

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:47.200
like you’re making a narrative stone soup.&nbsp;

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:48.200
You’re like “Here’s the pebble…”

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.379
Do you know the story of stone soup?&nbsp;

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:52.069
They go around and get the meat and the carrots
and everything.&nbsp;

00:03:52.069 --> 00:03:57.349
Then it’s more like they found art by just
pulling all of the things from your life:&nbsp;

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:00.560
your memories, what’s going on, and you’re
kind of building it out from this little thing

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:08.270
and it becomes something where you really
didn’t have much of an idea of what it was

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:11.580
going to be at all and so that’s pretty
fun.&nbsp;

00:04:11.580 --> 00:04:19.209
And that would again be an example of figuring
it out as you go.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:24.650
&nbsp;Question for the viewers:&nbsp; Do you figure
out your story and then write it?&nbsp;

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:28.930
Or do you figure out your story as you write
it?

